86492561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492814 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492814 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492814 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492814 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492814 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492814 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186492589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492814 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fb1325f4-cc8e-5bd1-b83f-e768c4904fe9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492839 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492839 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94252" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492839 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492839 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & \\,∗\\, & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & \\,∗\\, & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,∗\\, & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492839 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "20edcd99-4d6d-5145-9c47-d9e4447f46d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492839 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the second column must be orthogonal to the first and third columns, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492844 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492844 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94346" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492844 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492844 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first and second columns of the matrix $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492844 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2f400860-7ffc-5147-852c-7cca9efa6b2f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492844 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=7.$ So, the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ Computing $A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n\n$$\n{e}_{1}={v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand we find ${e}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{{v}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{{e}_{1}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{e}_{1} \\\\  & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, dividing ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ by their respective norms, we obtain the first and second columns of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{e}_{1}}{‖{e}_{1}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ \\frac{{e}_{2}}{‖{e}_{2}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492863 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492863 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140837" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492863 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492863 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492863 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5d11f87e-0592-5806-9319-d12902b8a14a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492863 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ So, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm, we obtain the first column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492884 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492884 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140829" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492884 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492884 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{2}{3} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\,∗\\, \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & \\,∗\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$ for some diagonal matrix $D,$ which of the following could be the third column of the matrix $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492884 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "94cf2153-a12b-5a66-9939-44bd440747ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492884 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nSince  $A=PD{P}^{T}$ is an orthogonal diagonalization of a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A,$ the matrix $P$ must be a $3\\times 3$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the third column must be orthogonal to the first and second columns, and\n\n- the norm of the third column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492889 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492889 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140843" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492889 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492889 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second and third columns of the matrix $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492889 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9e34d3f6-344a-52de-9e80-5526338f9855" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492889 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ So, the second and third columns of $P$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}.$ \n\nNow, we need to find eigenvectors that correspond to ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ Computing $A-4I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives two eigenvectors: \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\nNotice that eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are  orthogonal. So, we divide ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ by their respective norms to obtain the second and third columns of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ \\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492913 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492913 #edb/ref 1069 "q-94297" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492913 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492913 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. \nGiven that $D={P}^{T}\\,AP,$ where $P$ is an orthogonal matrix, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492913 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d9482400-abcf-59ec-ba2d-fb58a274b62b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492913 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PD{P}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Equivalently,\n\n$$\nD={P}^{T}AP.\n$$\n\nMoreover, a matrix $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.\n\nFirst, note that $A$ is symmetric and $D$ is diagonal. Therefore, $D={P}^{T}\\,AP$ gives an orthogonal diagonalization of $A.$\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=0,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=8.$ So, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=0.$ \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=0.$ Computing $A-0I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2}.$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm, we obtain the second column of $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
